er travel season on  our doorstep chris  all right abigail thank you that's  abigail beeman in ottawa  just ahead a golden opportunity for  canada at the summit of the americas  opportunity  prime minister justin trudeau is in the  u.s this week for president joe biden's  summit of the americas in los angeles  and while some countries weren't invited  and others most notably mexico have  opted out analysts say canada is in a  position to strengthen its presence  across the region david aiken is  following the prime minister's trip and  david trudeau's first stop is at norad  headquarters in colorado  chris prime minister justin trudeau and  his defense minister anita anand will be  on the ground at peterson space force  base in colorado springs for just a few  hours pmo officials say there's no  specific announcement associated with  this visit but the prime minister will  be briefed by military officials on  norad's activities and much needed norad  modernization  continental defense and in fact  hemispheric defense is increasingly top  of mind for canada and its allies these  days  russia's invasion of ukraine and  russia's subsequent threats to use  nuclear weapons have heightened those  concerns  indeed one of trudeau's key objectives  when he meets with leaders from the 32  other member nations this week is to  build support in central and south  america for a strong response against  russia so far not everyone in the region  mexico for example has condemned  russia's actions we see an opportunity  analysts also say that with the relative  decline in the united states influence  in south and central america canada has  a golden opportunity to play a bigger  role in helping others with everything  from adapting to climate change to  strengthening democracy and human rights  given the us position on all of this  only makes  canada's potential  role in the hemisphere  not just materially but symbolically  really critical right i mean latin  americans who are struggling for  democracy and human rights they want to  know that there's a there's a big  country in the neighborhood that's on  their side we could be so much more  if we just had the political will  and the interest on the part of the  canadian government to really increase  canada's stature and presence and  reputation in the region and they want  us to play a role now while he's in los  angeles the prime minister is going to  have a meeting with the california  governor gavin newsom the two men are  both progressive politicians and we  expect that they will have an  announcement on a climate change  agreement the pm then heads back home  saturday chris  david aiken in ottawa thanks david  up next families across two continents  united by a canadian war heroes  sacrifice  yesterday on the 78th anniversary of  d-day we introduced the story of harvey  edgar jones a canadian pilot who made  the ultimate sacrifice during the allied  invasion that continues to ripple  through the generations tonight dan  spector brings you part two of that  special story the dead of gratitude owed  and how decades later the son of this  soldier jones managed to save was  finally able to say thank you  the fire was so violent that the bombs  harvey engelberg feels his name bears  responsibility once i had my own kids i  started realizing  the heavy debt that that name gave me  his father cobby engelberg an rcaf radio  operator on a dakota transport plane  survived the aircraft being shot down by  the germans on d-day because pilot  harvey edgar jones stayed at the  controls instead of parachuting out like  the rest of the crew named after the  pilot who saved his father harvey  engelberg feels it's his duty to honor  his namesake i never met him but to be  named after somebody who died and you're  supposed to honor his memory  i mean that's a burden i mean a burden  it's a responsibility how's that but in  spite of his insatiable desire to show  jones's family gratitude he never had  luck tracking down his descendants or  the duhamels french farmers who pulled  his father from the plane's burning  wreckage after the dakota crashed on  their land engelberg has letters his  father exchanged with the duhamels in  later years i will be indebted to you  forever  how do you thank someone for saving your  life so imagine harvey engelberg's  surprise when a handwritten letter  arrived at his montreal home in march  from bassinville france she said i have  pieces of your father's plane would you  like them and then i immediately say  yeah i'm coming to see you  teres ferret and her husband now live on  that farm 78 years after that fateful  night the couple found pieces of dakota  kg 356 while working the land  in april engelberg visited the green  pasture where the plane went down it was  very emotional but it was just amazing  to see these strangers you know 6 000  miles away caring as much as me he  donated the shards of twisted metal to  the juno beach center canada's war  museum in normandy where they will soon  be displayed i honestly couldn't find a  better story for us to be telling  in our museum it was  although engelberg felt some peace his  work still felt unfinished he hadn't  been able to thank harvey edgar jones's  family i almost hit a wall i was trying  that's where we hoped we might be able  to help surely his relatives had to be  out there somewhere my goal in life was  to find out more about him that i could  and one day to thank his family  and here we are  helen peterson is harvey jones's niece  the daughter of his older sister she  knew him as uncle harv  he was my one and only uncle my favorite  uncle you might say finding out someone  was named in his honor she says is a  highlight of her life i'm just getting  all emotional of the whole thing and  just thinking how wonderful it is harvey  edgar jones was first laid to rest in a  well-maintained grave on the farm in  france but was reinterred at the  ranville war cemetery in normandy  alongside thousands of others who never  made it home he got a mention in  dispatches for his heroism but was never  awarded a higher military honor like the  victoria cross  probably the thing that stuck out to me  the most is just that nobody knows  hardly anybody knows his story  three families on two continents forever  connected by an act of bravery still  felt through the generations hope this  canadian hero's story gets a second look  dan specter global news montreal  thank you harvey  that is global national for this tuesday  night i'm chris galas tonight's york  canada shows the dandelions near la  stowell ontario we love seeing your  canada so please email your photos to  viewers at globalnational.com thanks  very much for watching donna friesen  will be back with you tomorrow hope you  have a great evening  [Music]  you
 

